< 47. **A key or action that unmounts the focused element names its focus target.** The task names the element that receives focus (normally the next Tab stop, run by B in the browser at the baseline) and C has a row for `document.activeElement` with a visible ring; "focus is not moved" is never the rule, and BODY is never an expected value. A focus-return row for a mouse-opened menu replays the real order in jsdom (right `pointerdown` → blur to BODY → `contextmenu`), and the browser recipe records `document.activeElement` after closing; a `contextMenu`-only test proves nothing about a mouse open. The recipe probes one origin per class that any press handler on the path blurs (a button, a range input, a typing field, a loop brace, a marker), not only a button. An editor that commits and unmounts on its own blur (grep `settleEditor('blur')`) names its **successor**, where Enter would land (the committed flag, the group name), recorded at the settle and used only for that input. Evidence: GATE232-S1 (the round-232 task wrote BODY into C-TOGGLE-FOCUS; gate 232 rated it Medium); C-R241-01 (GM11/GM18 green since SRW-14, BODY in Chromium); C-R242-01 (round 242 probed only the Snap toggle; the capture-phase edit-focus release still lost the fader); B-R242-MARKER-EDITOR-MENU-FOCUS + B-R243-GROUP-EDITOR-MENU-FOCUS (the round-243 recipe filed one editor and missed the second) (4×).
